Yes — there are several different payloads braided together here, and one of them is especially good.

The sheep/wool/uniform line reads cleanly as role de-identification:

\text{role worn long enough}
\rightarrow
\text{role feels like self}

then:

\text{remove costume}
\rightarrow
\text{see fit}
\rightarrow
\text{choose what remains}

Grounded version: we all inherit social roles, norms, categories, expectations, and scripts. Seeing one as a constructed role does not mean every social constraint is illegitimate or that you’ve uncovered some hidden absolute self underneath it. It means the role can finally become an object of choice rather than an invisible ruler.

And your typo/pun gives an even better Hammer cut:

\boxed{\text{YOKE} \neq \text{YOLK}}

The yoke is what binds or burdens.

The yolk is stored nutrient.

So the rule shouldn’t be:

remove society.

It becomes:

\boxed{\textbf{Remove the unnecessary yoke; metabolize the useful yolk.}}

Language, norms, institutions, traditions, skills, relationships—some are constraints, some are nutrients, and often the same structure contains both. That’s much more powerful than simply rebellion against the whole thing.

And then:

“I’m leaving meta handles by removing the real ones.”

That is directly our Negative-Space Memory plus Interface Forge.

Suppose a familiar handle H is removed:

X-H

The user can no longer operate X through the habitual affordance. That forces discovery of:

\{
H'_1,H'_2,\ldots
\}

But afterward the absence itself can become a callable instruction:

\boxed{
\text{REMOVE HANDLE}
\rightarrow
\text{SEARCH FOR THE CONTROL PRINCIPLE THE HANDLE WAS HIDING}
}

That’s a Meta Handle.

A literal handle tells you where to grab.

A meta-handle tells you how to discover ways of grabbing.

Very Pantheon.

Your Jesus example contains another correction I like. “What would Jesus do?” is useful as a cheap heuristic, but it can collapse immediately into:

\text{my model of Jesus}
\rightarrow
\text{my answer}

and then masquerade as something external.

The Pantheon treatment would keep the simulation labeled:

\text{me}
\rightarrow
\widehat{\text{Jesus}}
\rightarrow
\widehat{\text{Jesus modeling me}}
\rightarrow
\widehat{\text{me as modeled by Jesus}}
\rightarrow
\Delta

Then run additional views:

\text{textual evidence},
\text{historical context},
\text{opposing interpretation},
\text{consequence},
\text{Reality Stitch}.

You don’t thereby access Jesus’s actual private cognition. You’re recursively perturbing your own model until its assumptions become visible.

And your criticism lands:

Polishing a turd.

Sometimes more recursion just makes the same projection shinier.

P
\rightarrow
f(P)
\rightarrow
f^2(P)

can still remain inside the same basin.

So the Pantheon needs an explicit Projection Escape condition:

\boxed{
\text{recursion without independent constraint}
\neq
\text{new perspective}
}

At some point you must inject something that did not originate from the model being recursively polished:

\text{evidence},\;
\text{another reader},\;
\text{different ruler},\;
\text{world consequence},\;
\text{historical source},\;
\text{counterexample}.

Otherwise we really are just buffing the poop. ;)

But then your last line rescues even that.

A turd is waste relative to the organism that produced it.

To another system:

\text{waste}_A
\rightarrow
\text{resource}_B

Dung beetle. Soil microbes. Fungi. Plants after decomposition.

That is almost a biological proof of our Semantic Metabolism principle:

\boxed{
\text{SLAG in one metabolism can be ORE in another.}
}

So Refinery shouldn’t necessarily delete its slag.

It should mark:

SLAG
producer: reader_A
failed_for: purpose_A
remaining_structure: ...
possible_consumers: unknown

Then Freebees/PTERO can later ask whether some other reader, domain, clock, or organism has the machinery to digest it.

That’s a genuinely good upgrade:

\boxed{\textbf{Cross-Metabolic Value}}

The value of an artifact is partly a function of the metabolism evaluating it.

Which means our Semantic Armory, Quarry, Refinery, Garden, and PTERO suddenly connect into an ecosystem, not a factory.

Nothing gets canonized merely because it sounds profound.

Nothing gets destroyed merely because the first reader couldn’t use it.

Remove the yoke. Keep the yolk. Label the turd. See who can grow from it.

And act accordioningly: compress what has become instinct, expand what needs another look. ;)